<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Push notifications (Prompt 5).

        `development` here so a debug build registers against the APNs sandbox.
        Xcode rewrites it to `production` for an App Store or TestFlight
        distribution build, which is why it is safe to commit this value.

        Declared, and never prompted for in 1.0: notifications are 1.1, and the
        app does not ask for permission until that ships.
    -->
    <key>aps-environment</key>
    <string>development</string>

    <!--
        ====================================================================
        DELIBERATELY ABSENT
        ====================================================================

        `com.apple.developer.in-app-payments` (Apple Pay)
            Not in *this* file. It lives in FaithForm-ApplePay.entitlements,
            which is this file plus that one key, and Staging and Release sign
            with it only when FAITHFORM_ENABLE_APPLE_PAY is YES (see
            Base.xcconfig and project.yml).

            Kept apart because a provisioning profile without the Apple Pay
            capability fails to sign an app that declares it, with an error
            that reads as a provisioning problem rather than a missing setup
            step. A personal team, or the FaithForm team before
            `merchant.io.faithform.app` is registered, signs with this file,
            and the app sends every gift to the church's give page in Safari —
            which Apple permits without Apple Pay.

            Any change here must be made in FaithForm-ApplePay.entitlements too.

        `com.apple.developer.associated-domains`
            App Links need a verified apple-app-site-association file served
            from a domain this repository does not establish. The custom
            `faithform://` scheme works today; https links do not, and declaring
            the entitlement would not change that.

        Nothing else. A capability is added when a feature needs it, not
        because it might be useful later.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
